"""Saint-of-the-day entries for Ora, August (original text). See tools/SAINTS_BRIEF.md."""
SAINTS_NEW = {
 "08-01": dict(name="Saint Alphonsus Liguori", dates="1696–1787 · Naples and Pagani", match=["alphonsus"],
  history="A brilliant Neapolitan lawyer who left the courts after losing a case through his own oversight, Alphonsus became a priest, founded the Redemptorists to preach to the neglected poor of the countryside, and wrote on moral theology with a gentleness that changed how confessors treated penitents. He is a Doctor of the Church and patron of confessors.",
  life="Alphonsus took his doctorate in law at sixteen and practised with great success until 1723, when he lost an important case through a point he had overlooked. He took it as a sign, laid his sword before a statue of Our Lady and was ordained in 1726. Preaching in the hills above Amalfi, he found shepherds and peasants who had no one to teach them, and in 1732 at Scala he founded the Congregation of the Most Holy Redeemer. His Moral Theology steered between rigour and laxity and taught confessors to be fathers rather than judges. He also wrote The Glories of Mary and Visits to the Blessed Sacrament, and the Christmas carol Tu scendi dalle stelle. He was bishop of Sant’Agata dei Goti from 1762 to 1775. In old age, crippled and nearly blind, he was shut out of his own congregation through a misunderstanding, and bore it patiently. He died at Pagani in 1787.",
  prayerTo="Saint Alphonsus, you spent yourself for the poor and the forgotten, and taught confessors to be merciful. Pray for me when I am weighed down by scruples or discouraged by my sins, that I may trust in the mercy of the Redeemer. Help me to visit Him often in the Blessed Sacrament and to love His Mother as you did. Amen.",
  penance="Make a short visit to the Blessed Sacrament today, as Alphonsus urged, and if it has been a while, plan your next confession.",
  meditation="Alphonsus wrote that whoever prays is certainly saved, and whoever does not pray is certainly lost. Is there a set time in your day that belongs to God alone?",
  query="Saint Alphonsus Liguori painting"),
 "08-02": dict(name="Saint Peter Julian Eymard", dates="1811–1868 · La Mure and Paris", match=["eymard"],
  history="A French priest of the Alps whose life was drawn ever more strongly to the Eucharist. After years as a Marist he founded the Congregation of the Blessed Sacrament in Paris in 1856, a community of priests and brothers devoted to perpetual adoration, and a sister congregation of women. He is remembered as the apostle of the Eucharist.",
  life="Peter Julian was born at La Mure, near Grenoble, the son of a cutler. His father opposed his vocation and his health was poor, but he was ordained for the diocese of Grenoble in 1834 and five years later joined the newly founded Society of Mary. As a Marist superior at Lyon he came to believe that Christ in the Eucharist deserved a community wholly given to Him. With the approval of the Archbishop of Paris he founded the Congregation of the Blessed Sacrament in Paris in 1856, and in 1858, with Marguerite Guillot, the Servants of the Blessed Sacrament. He began perpetual exposition in Paris, prepared poor adult workers for their First Communion, and wrote much on prayer before the Blessed Sacrament. Worn out, he died in his home town on 1 August 1868. John XXIII canonised him in 1962, during the Second Vatican Council.",
  prayerTo="Saint Peter Julian, you found in the Eucharist the centre of your life and longed for Christ to be adored in every place. Pray that I may come to Him often, kneel before Him with faith, and receive Him with love. Teach me to make my whole day an act of thanksgiving. Amen.",
  penance="Spend half an hour in adoration today, or a few quiet minutes before a tabernacle, and offer it for priests.",
  meditation="He taught that adoration is not idleness but the most fruitful work of all. What would it mean to bring one real concern of today to Christ in the tabernacle and simply leave it there?",
  query="Peter Julian Eymard portrait"),
 "08-04": dict(name="Saint John Vianney", dates="1786–1859 · Dardilly and Ars", match=["vianney"],
  history="A farmer’s son who struggled with his studies and was nearly refused ordination, John Vianney was sent in 1818 to Ars, a small village near Lyon where faith had grown cold. By prayer, penance and long hours in the confessional he transformed it, and pilgrims came from all over France to confess to him. He is the patron of parish priests.",
  life="Jean-Marie Vianney grew up in the years of the Revolution, when Mass was said in secret, and made his First Communion behind shuttered windows. He began his studies late and found Latin almost impossible; the patient Abbé Balley tutored him, and he was ordained in 1815. In 1818 he became curé of Ars, a village of a little over two hundred people. He visited every family, catechised the children, preached against drunkenness and Sunday work, and spent hours before the tabernacle. In time he heard confessions for eleven hours a day and more, and by his later years some twenty thousand pilgrims a year came to Ars. Several times he tried to slip away to a life of solitude, and each time he returned. He lived on very little and gave away everything he had. He died in 1859, was canonised in 1925 and named patron of parish priests in 1929.",
  prayerTo="Saint John Vianney, you gave your life to the souls of one small parish and to all who came to you for mercy. Pray for my parish priest and for all priests, that they may be holy, and for me, that I may make a good confession and return to God with my whole heart. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for your parish priest by name today, and if you can, do something practical for him or for your parish.",
  meditation="An old peasant told the curé what he did during his long visits in church: I look at Him and He looks at me. Sit before Christ for five minutes today and let yourself be looked at.",
  query="Jean-Marie Vianney portrait"),
 "08-05": dict(name="Dedication of the Basilica of Saint Mary Major", dates="Rome, fifth century", match=["major"], efMatch="Mary Major",
  history="Soon after the Council of Ephesus proclaimed Mary the Mother of God in 431, Pope Sixtus III built a great basilica in her honour on the Esquiline Hill. It is among the oldest churches in the West dedicated to Our Lady and one of the four major basilicas of Rome. A later legend tells of snow falling there in August, so it is also called Our Lady of the Snows.",
  life="The basilica of Santa Maria Maggiore keeps much of its fifth-century form. The mosaics of Sixtus III still shine along the nave and over the triumphal arch, telling the story of the Old Testament and the childhood of Christ, and proclaiming the faith of Ephesus that Mary’s Son is truly God. A medieval legend says that in the time of Pope Liberius a Roman couple and the pope dreamed that Our Lady wished a church built where snow would fall, and on the morning of 5 August snow lay on the Esquiline. Each year white petals are let fall from the ceiling in memory of it. The basilica keeps the relic venerated as the wood of the crib of Bethlehem and the ancient icon of Our Lady called Salus Populi Romani. Several popes are buried there, among them Saint Pius V and, in 2025, Pope Francis.",
  prayerTo="Holy Mary, Mother of God, the people of Rome built a house for you when the Church first proclaimed your dignity. Make my heart a dwelling where your Son is welcome. Protect the Church, guard our families, and lead us to the house of the Father, where you wait for us. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Rosary today, or one decade, for the Holy Father and for your own parish church.",
  meditation="The Church honours a building because of the One born of Mary. Where in your life have you made room for Christ, and where is there still no room at the inn?",
  query="Founding of Santa Maria Maggiore miracle of the snow painting Masolino"),
 "08-06": dict(name="The Transfiguration of the Lord", dates="Feast of the Lord", match=["transfiguration"], efMatch="Transfiguration",
  history="Christ took Peter, James and John up a high mountain, by tradition Mount Tabor, and was transfigured before them: His face shone like the sun and His clothes became white as light. Moses and Elijah spoke with Him of His coming death, and the Father’s voice said, This is my beloved Son; listen to Him.",
  life="All three synoptic Gospels tell of the Transfiguration, and Saint Peter recalls it in his second letter: we were eyewitnesses of His majesty, when we were with Him on the holy mountain. It came soon after Christ first foretold His Passion, and the Fathers saw in it a gift to strengthen the apostles for the scandal of the cross. Moses and Elijah stand for the Law and the Prophets, both fulfilled in Him. Peter, not knowing what he said, wanted to build three tents and stay. The feast was kept in the East from early centuries. In 1457 Pope Callixtus III extended it to the whole Western Church, in thanksgiving for the relief of Belgrade the year before, news of which had reached Rome on 6 August.",
  prayerTo="Lord Jesus, transfigured on the mountain, you showed your friends your glory before they saw you suffer. When my faith is tested by darkness and pain, let me remember the light of your face. Help me to listen to you, as the Father commands, and bring me at last to see you as you are. Amen.",
  penance="Climb a hill or find a high place today if you can, and pray there; otherwise read Matthew 17:1–9 slowly and in silence.",
  meditation="It is good for us to be here, said Peter. Jesus led them back down the mountain. Where is He asking you to carry the light of prayer into ordinary work today?",
  query="Transfiguration painting Raphael"),
 "08-07": dict(name="Saint Cajetan", dates="1480–1547 · Vicenza, Rome and Naples", match=["cajetan"], efMatch="Cajetan",
  history="A nobleman of Vicenza and a lawyer in the papal court, Cajetan became a priest and gave his life to reforming the clergy from within. In 1524 he founded the Theatines, priests who lived in poverty and trusted in Providence alone, refusing even to beg. He served the sick and the poor, and is the patron of those seeking work.",
  life="Gaetano dei Conti di Thiene took his doctorate in law at Padua and went to Rome to serve Pope Julius II. There he joined the Oratory of Divine Love, a confraternity of priests and laymen devoted to prayer and to care for the incurable sick, and was ordained in 1516. Grieved by the worldliness of many clergy, in 1524 he founded with Gian Pietro Carafa, the future Pope Paul IV, the Clerks Regular, called Theatines. They lived in strict poverty, owned nothing, did not beg, and relied on what Providence sent, while preaching, celebrating the liturgy well and caring for the sick. During the Sack of Rome in 1527 Cajetan was beaten by soldiers who wanted money he did not have. In Naples he worked among the poor and helped found a lending house to rescue them from usurers. He died there in 1547 and was canonised in 1671.",
  prayerTo="Saint Cajetan, you trusted the Father to feed His children and gave your life to the poor and the sick. Pray for all who are out of work or anxious about money, that they may find honest work and daily bread. Free me from worry, and teach me to seek first the Kingdom of God. Amen.",
  penance="Give something today to a food bank or a charity that helps people into work, and pray for someone you know who is looking for a job.",
  meditation="Cajetan would not let his priests beg, because he believed God already knew what they needed. What would change if you truly believed your Father knows what you need before you ask?",
  query="Saint Cajetan painting"),
 "08-08": dict(name="Saint Dominic", dates="c. 1170–1221 · Castile, Languedoc and Bologna", match=["dominic"],
  history="A Castilian canon who, travelling through southern France, saw how the Cathar heresy had won people from the Church, and how little the faith was being preached. He answered with poverty, study and preaching, and founded the Order of Preachers, approved in 1216. His friars spread quickly to the universities and cities of Europe.",
  life="Dominic de Guzmán was born at Caleruega in Castile and studied at Palencia, where in a time of famine he sold his precious books to feed the hungry. As a canon of Osma he travelled with his bishop, Diego, through Languedoc, where the Cathars taught that the material world was evil. Dominic saw that the people respected their preachers’ austere lives, and resolved to preach the Gospel in the same poverty, on foot and begging his bread. In 1206 he gathered converted women into a community at Prouille, the first of his nuns. In 1216 Pope Honorius III approved his order, and Dominic soon sent his small band of friars to Paris, Bologna and beyond, to study and to preach. Those who knew him said he spoke only with God or about God, and that he was cheerful by day and wept in prayer by night. He died at Bologna in 1221 and was canonised in 1234.",
  prayerTo="Saint Dominic, you gave your life to preach the truth with charity and joy. Pray that I may love the truth enough to study it and live it, and speak of God with gentleness to those who do not know Him. Win for the Church preachers who are poor, learned and holy. Amen.",
  penance="Spend twenty minutes today reading Scripture or a good book of the faith, and pray one decade of the Rosary, which tradition links with Dominic.",
  meditation="Dominic’s friars were to contemplate and to hand on to others the fruits of contemplation. What have you received from God lately that you could share with someone else?",
  query="Saint Dominic painting Fra Angelico"),
 "08-09": dict(name="Saint Teresa Benedicta of the Cross", dates="1891–1942 · Breslau, Cologne and Auschwitz", match=["benedicta", "stein"],
  history="Edith Stein, a brilliant Jewish philosopher, found the truth she had long sought in reading the life of Saint Teresa of Ávila and was baptised in 1922. She became a Carmelite nun in Cologne. In 1942 she was arrested in the Netherlands and killed at Auschwitz. She is a co-patron of Europe.",
  life="Edith Stein was born in Breslau on the Day of Atonement, 1891, the youngest child of a devout Jewish family. As a young woman she lost her faith, studied philosophy and became the assistant of Edmund Husserl. In 1921, staying with friends, she read the autobiography of Saint Teresa of Ávila through the night and, closing it, knew that it was the truth. She was baptised on 1 January 1922. For years she taught at a Dominican school in Speyer, lectured widely and wrote on women and education. Barred from teaching by the Nazi laws of 1933, she entered the Carmel of Cologne that year. In 1938 she was moved to Echt in the Netherlands for safety. After the Dutch bishops publicly condemned the deportation of Jews, the Nazis arrested Catholics of Jewish origin, and on 2 August 1942 Edith and her sister Rosa were taken. She died in the gas chamber at Auschwitz on 9 August. John Paul II canonised her in 1998.",
  prayerTo="Saint Teresa Benedicta, you sought the truth with your whole mind and found it in Christ crucified. Pray for all who search, for the Jewish people you loved, and for Europe, whose patron you are. Help me to embrace the cross that comes to me and to stand with those who suffer. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the Jewish people and for all victims of hatred, and give an hour to serious reading that will help you understand your faith more deeply.",
  meditation="As she was taken, Edith is remembered to have said to her sister, Come, we are going for our people. For whom is God asking you to stand today?"),
 "08-10": dict(name="Saint Lawrence", dates="Died 258 · Rome", match=["lawrence"], efMatch="Lawrence",
  history="One of the seven deacons of Rome, Lawrence cared for the Church’s goods and its poor. He was martyred in 258 under the Emperor Valerian, a few days after his bishop, Pope Sixtus II. His courage made him one of the best-loved martyrs of Rome, and his name is said in the Roman Canon.",
  life="When Valerian ordered the execution of bishops, priests and deacons, Pope Sixtus II was beheaded on 6 August 258. Tradition, recorded by Saint Ambrose, says that Lawrence followed the pope to his death, asking why he should be left behind, and that Sixtus told him he would follow in three days. The prefect of Rome demanded the treasures of the Church. Lawrence asked for time, gathered the poor, the sick and the widows whom the Church supported, and presented them: these are the treasure of the Church. Tradition holds that he was roasted on a gridiron and, with astonishing calm, told his executioners to turn him over, for one side was done. He was buried on the Via Tiburtina, where the basilica of San Lorenzo fuori le Mura now stands. The Perseid meteors of early August are called in Italy the tears of Saint Lawrence.",
  prayerTo="Saint Lawrence, deacon and martyr, you saw the face of Christ in the poor and called them the treasure of the Church. Pray that I may serve them gladly and not be ashamed of them. Give me some of your courage and good humour when I suffer for what is right. Amen.",
  penance="Give generously to the poor today, in money, food or time, as Lawrence gave the Church’s goods away rather than see them seized.",
  meditation="These are the treasure of the Church. Who are the poor in your own street or parish, and do you see them as treasure or as a burden?",
  query="Martyrdom of Saint Lawrence painting Titian"),
 "08-11": dict(name="Saint Clare", dates="1194–1253 · Assisi", match=["clare"],
  history="A young noblewoman of Assisi who, moved by the preaching of Saint Francis, left her family by night in 1212 to give herself to Christ in poverty. At San Damiano she founded the Poor Ladies, now the Poor Clares, and for over forty years guarded their poverty with great firmness. She wrote the first rule for women approved by a pope.",
  life="Clare Offreduccio heard Francis preach and resolved to follow Christ as he did. On the night after Palm Sunday 1212 she slipped out of her family’s house and went to the little church of the Portiuncula, where Francis cut her hair and received her vows. Her family tried to bring her back by force, but she held to the altar. Her sister Agnes soon joined her, and later her widowed mother. At San Damiano the sisters lived enclosed, in strict poverty, by prayer and the work of their hands. Clare fought for years for the privilege of owning nothing at all, and her own rule was approved by Innocent IV two days before she died. Tradition holds that when soldiers attacked the convent in 1240 she faced them with the Blessed Sacrament and they fled. Too ill to go to Mass one Christmas, she is said to have seen and heard it from her bed, and so is patron of television. She was canonised in 1255.",
  prayerTo="Saint Clare, you held fast to Christ poor and crucified and found in Him all your riches. Pray that I may be free of the things I cling to, and find my joy in God. Watch over the Poor Clares and all enclosed nuns, and make my own home a place of prayer and peace. Amen.",
  penance="Give away something you own and do not need today, and pray for an enclosed community of nuns near you.",
  meditation="Clare urged Saint Agnes of Prague to gaze upon Christ, consider Him and contemplate Him. Spend a few minutes today simply looking at a crucifix or an image of Christ.",
  query="Saint Clare painting Simone Martini"),
 "08-12": dict(name="Saint Jane Frances de Chantal", dates="1572–1641 · Dijon and Annecy", match=["chantal"],
  history="A Burgundian baroness, wife and mother, widowed at twenty-eight when her husband was killed in a hunting accident. Under the direction of Saint Francis de Sales she grew in holiness and in 1610 founded with him the Order of the Visitation at Annecy, open to women whose age or health kept them from stricter orders.",
  life="Jane Frémyot married Christophe de Rabutin, Baron de Chantal, in 1592, and they were happy together. Four of their children survived infancy. In 1601 Christophe was shot by accident while hunting and died, and Jane, grief-stricken, struggled for years to forgive the man responsible, until at last she stood godmother to his child. In 1604 she heard Francis de Sales preach in Dijon and placed herself under his direction. He taught her a gentle, steady holiness in place of anxiety. In 1610 they founded the Visitation at Annecy. Leaving home was hard; her teenage son, the story goes, lay across the threshold to stop her, and she stepped over him in tears. The order spread quickly, and by her death there were over eighty houses. After Francis died in 1622, Saint Vincent de Paul became her friend and counsellor. She died at Moulins in 1641 and was canonised in 1767.",
  prayerTo="Saint Jane Frances, you knew the joys of marriage and the grief of losing your husband, and you learned to forgive. Pray for widows and for all who mourn, for those who cannot yet forgive, and for me, that I may serve God with a steady and gentle heart. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for someone who has hurt you today and, if it is safe and right, take one small step towards reconciliation.",
  meditation="Francis de Sales taught her to do everything by love and nothing by force. Where are you trying to force holiness instead of letting God lead you gently?",
  query="Saint Jane Frances de Chantal painting"),
 "08-13": dict(name="Saints Pontian and Hippolytus", dates="Died c. 235 · Rome and Sardinia", match=["pontian", "hippolytus"],
  history="Pontian was Bishop of Rome and Hippolytus a learned Roman priest who had set himself up as a rival bishop. In 235 the Emperor Maximinus sent them both to the mines of Sardinia, where they died. Tradition holds that they were reconciled there, and both are honoured as martyrs.",
  life="Hippolytus was the most important theologian of the Roman Church in the early third century, writing in Greek on Scripture and against the heresies of his time. He thought Pope Callistus too lenient towards repentant sinners and too unclear in his teaching, broke with him and was chosen bishop by a group of followers, the first antipope we know of. The schism lasted into the time of Pope Pontian. When Maximinus Thrax turned on the Church’s leaders in 235, he exiled both men to the mines of Sardinia, where few survived. Pontian resigned his office so that Rome could elect a successor, the first pope known to have done so. Hippolytus is believed to have returned to unity with the Church and urged his followers to do the same. Both died in exile, and Pope Fabian brought their bodies back to Rome to be buried with honour.",
  prayerTo="Saints Pontian and Hippolytus, you were divided in life and united in suffering for Christ. Pray for all who are estranged within the Church and among Christians, that we may be reconciled before it is too late. Help me to put the unity of the Church above my own opinions. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the unity of Christians, and make peace with someone in your parish or family with whom you have quarrelled.",
  meditation="A rival bishop and a pope died side by side in the mines. Is there a disagreement you are holding on to that you could lay down for the sake of peace?",
  query="Martyrdom of Saint Hippolytus painting Dieric Bouts"),
 "08-14": dict(name="Saint Maximilian Kolbe", dates="1894–1941 · Poland, Japan and Auschwitz", match=["kolbe"],
  history="A Polish Franciscan who spread devotion to the Immaculate Virgin through the press, founding a great friary and publishing house near Warsaw and a mission in Japan. Imprisoned at Auschwitz, he offered his life in place of a condemned man with a family and died in the starvation bunker. He is honoured as a martyr of charity.",
  life="Raymund Kolbe was born at Zduńska Wola. As a boy, he said, Our Lady showed him two crowns, one white for purity and one red for martyrdom, and he accepted both. He joined the Conventual Franciscans, taking the name Maximilian, and in Rome in 1917 founded the Militia of the Immaculata. Back in Poland, despite tuberculosis, he built Niepokalanów, the City of the Immaculata, where hundreds of friars printed magazines read across the country. From 1930 he founded a similar mission at Nagasaki. Arrested by the Gestapo in 1941, he was sent to Auschwitz. That summer, after a prisoner escaped, ten men were chosen to die by starvation. When one, Franciszek Gajowniczek, cried out for his wife and children, Maximilian stepped forward and asked to take his place. He led the others in prayer and hymns for two weeks and was killed by lethal injection on 14 August. Gajowniczek survived and was present when John Paul II canonised him in 1982.",
  prayerTo="Saint Maximilian, you gave your life for a stranger out of love for Christ and His Mother. Pray for all who suffer in prisons and camps, for families torn apart, and for me, that I may give myself in small ways each day, so that I am ready to give everything if God should ask. Amen.",
  penance="Make a small sacrifice for someone else today, giving up your place, your time or your preference, and pray for prisoners.",
  meditation="Hatred is not a creative force; only love is creative, he is remembered as saying. Where is love asking you to take someone else’s place, even in a small thing?"),
 "08-15": dict(name="The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", dates="Dogma defined 1950", match=["assumption"], efMatch="Assumption",
  history="The Church teaches that the Immaculate Mother of God, the ever-virgin Mary, having completed the course of her earthly life, was taken up body and soul into heavenly glory. Pope Pius XII defined this as a dogma of faith on 1 November 1950. It is one of the oldest feasts of Our Lady and a holy day of obligation.",
  life="Scripture does not describe the end of Mary’s life, but the Church in both East and West has long believed that she who bore Christ was not left to the corruption of the grave. In the East the feast of the Dormition, the falling asleep of the Mother of God, was kept by the sixth century, and the Emperor Maurice fixed it on 15 August. Rome adopted it in the seventh century. Pius XII, after consulting the bishops of the whole world, defined the dogma in the apostolic constitution Munificentissimus Deus. The definition does not settle whether Mary died before she was assumed, though the long tradition of East and West speaks of her falling asleep. The feast proclaims what God has done for Mary and promises what He will do for all the faithful: the resurrection of the body and life with God in body and soul.",
  prayerTo="Holy Mary, taken up into heaven, you are the sign of hope for the pilgrim Church. Look on us who still struggle on earth. Keep our eyes on the glory to which God calls us, help us to honour our bodies as temples of the Holy Spirit, and bring us home to your Son. Amen.",
  penance="Go to Mass today, a holy day of obligation, and pray the Glorious Mysteries of the Rosary, perhaps with your family.",
  meditation="Where Mary has gone, the Church hopes to follow. How would you live today if you truly believed your body, too, is made for glory?",
  query="Assumption of the Virgin painting Titian"),
 "08-16": dict(name="Saint Stephen of Hungary", dates="c. 975–1038 · Hungary", match=["hungary"],
  history="The first king of Hungary, crowned around the year 1000. Baptised as a boy, Stephen built the Church in his country, founded dioceses and monasteries, gave laws shaped by the Gospel and cared personally for the poor. Before he died he entrusted his kingdom to the Virgin Mary.",
  life="Stephen was born Vajk, son of Géza, chief prince of the Magyars, a people who had settled in the Carpathian basin a century before. He was baptised as a boy and married Gisela, sister of the future Emperor Saint Henry II. When Géza died, Stephen put down rebellion and in about the year 1000 was crowned king, by tradition with a crown sent by Pope Sylvester II. He founded the archdiocese of Esztergom and other sees, built churches, supported the abbey of Pannonhalma and welcomed missionaries. His laws protected widows and orphans, and he was known to give alms in disguise. His deep grief was the death of his only surviving son, Saint Emeric, in a hunting accident in 1031. Having no heir, Stephen offered Hungary to Our Lady. He died on the feast of the Assumption in 1038 and was canonised in 1083. His right hand is venerated in Budapest.",
  prayerTo="Saint Stephen, you ruled your people as a Christian king and entrusted your kingdom to the Mother of God. Pray for all who govern, that they may seek justice and protect the weak. Pray for parents who have lost children, and for me, that I may give God the first place in all I do. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for those who hold public office, and give alms quietly to someone in need.",
  meditation="In the counsels he wrote for his son, Stephen urged him to be patient and merciful, and to rule as a servant of Christ. Whom has God placed in your care, and how are you serving them?",
  query="Saint Stephen of Hungary painting"),
 "08-17": dict(name="Saint Hyacinth", dates="c. 1185–1257 · Kraków", match=["hyacinth"], efMatch="Hyacinth",
  history="A Polish Dominican who received the habit in the first years of the order and carried its preaching across Poland and far to the north and east. He founded houses of friars in Kraków and beyond and was called the apostle of the North. He died in Kraków on the feast of the Assumption, 1257.",
  life="Jacek Odrowąż was a priest of a noble Silesian family. Around 1220 he went to Rome with his bishop and there, tradition holds, received the habit of the Friars Preachers from Saint Dominic himself. Returning to Poland, he founded the Dominican house of the Holy Trinity in Kraków and others in Gdańsk and further afield. He preached in Poland, Prussia and among the peoples of the eastern lands, travelling enormous distances on foot. Many stories gathered around him. The best known says that when the Tatars attacked Kyiv he rescued the Blessed Sacrament from the church and, hearing Our Lady ask not to be left behind, carried off her heavy statue as well, which became light in his arms. He had a great love for the Mother of God and died, fittingly, on the day of her Assumption. He was canonised in 1594.",
  prayerTo="Saint Hyacinth, tireless preacher, you carried Christ and His Mother to people who had not heard of them. Pray for missionaries, for Poland, and for all who travel for the sake of the Gospel. When I am in danger, help me to keep hold of what matters most. Amen.",
  penance="Walk somewhere today instead of driving, praying as you go for missionaries and for the conversion of those who do not know Christ.",
  meditation="The story says the statue of Our Lady became light when he lifted it. Which burden might become lighter if you carried it with her?",
  query="Vision of Saint Hyacinth painting Ludovico Carracci"),
 "08-19": dict(name="Saint John Eudes", dates="1601–1680 · Normandy", match=["eudes"], efMatch="Eudes",
  history="A Norman priest and tireless preacher of parish missions who founded a congregation to train priests in seminaries and another to care for women leaving prostitution. He was the first to compose liturgical feasts in honour of the Sacred Heart of Jesus and the Heart of Mary.",
  life="John Eudes joined the French Oratory under Cardinal de Bérulle and was ordained in 1625. When plague struck Normandy he nursed the sick, and in one outbreak lived apart, it is said in a large cask in a field, so as not to carry the infection to his brothers. For more than fifty years he preached missions across Normandy and beyond, often with a team of confessors. Convinced that the reform of the Church depended on well-formed priests, he left the Oratory in 1643 and founded the Congregation of Jesus and Mary, the Eudists, to run seminaries. He also founded the Order of Our Lady of Charity for women who wished to leave prostitution, from which the Good Shepherd Sisters later grew. He wrote Masses and Offices for the Heart of Mary and the Heart of Jesus, and kept them in his communities long before the universal Church did. He died at Caen in 1680 and was canonised in 1925.",
  prayerTo="Saint John Eudes, you loved the Hearts of Jesus and Mary and spent your life for priests, for sinners and for the sick. Pray for seminarians and all who form them, for women caught in exploitation, and for me, that my heart may be made like the heart of Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for seminarians and vocations, and support a charity that helps women escape trafficking or exploitation.",
  meditation="He taught that Christians should continue the life of Jesus in their own lives, as members of His body. In what one thing today could you let Christ live through you?",
  query="Saint John Eudes painting"),
 "08-20": dict(name="Saint Bernard of Clairvaux", dates="1090–1153 · Burgundy and Clairvaux", match=["bernard"], efMatch="Bernard",
  history="A young Burgundian noble who entered the struggling monastery of Cîteaux with about thirty relatives and friends, then became founding abbot of Clairvaux. Bernard was the most influential churchman of his age, a mystic, preacher and counsellor of popes. His sermons on the Song of Songs and on Our Lady earned him the title of the honey-sweet doctor.",
  life="Bernard arrived at Cîteaux in 1113, and his coming revived the new Cistercian reform. In 1115 he was sent to found Clairvaux, and from there some seventy monasteries were founded in his lifetime. He was drawn constantly into the affairs of the Church: he helped end the schism of 1130 on the side of Innocent II, advised his former monk, Pope Eugene III, and argued against Peter Abelard. At the pope’s request he preached the Second Crusade, whose failure grieved him deeply. When a wandering monk stirred up attacks on the Jews of the Rhineland, Bernard went there in person and stopped him. Yet his heart was in the cloister. He wrote of the love of God, of humility and of the Virgin Mary with great warmth, and Dante chose him as his last guide in Paradise. He died in 1153 and was named a Doctor of the Church in 1830.",
  prayerTo="Saint Bernard, you loved Christ with all your heart and taught the Church to look to Mary in every danger. Pray that in temptation and confusion I may look to the star and call upon her. Help me to love God for His own sake, and to love Him without measure. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Memorare slowly today, or a decade of the Rosary, for someone who has lost hope.",
  meditation="In dangers, in difficulties, in doubts, think of Mary, call upon Mary, Bernard preached. What doubt or difficulty will you bring to her today?",
  query="Vision of Saint Bernard painting Filippino Lippi"),
 "08-21": dict(name="Saint Pius X", dates="1835–1914 · Riese, Venice and Rome", match=["pius"],
  history="Giuseppe Sarto, the son of a village postman, was a parish priest for years before becoming bishop of Mantua, Patriarch of Venice and, in 1903, pope. His motto was to restore all things in Christ. He encouraged frequent Communion, allowed children to receive it from the age of reason, and renewed sacred music.",
  life="Giuseppe Sarto was born at Riese in the Veneto, one of ten children of a poor family. After ordination in 1858 he served as a curate and then a parish priest, teaching the catechism himself and caring for the sick in a cholera outbreak. He became bishop of Mantua in 1884 and Patriarch of Venice in 1893, and remained simple in his habits. Elected pope in 1903, he took as his programme Saint Paul’s words, to restore all things in Christ. He urged the faithful to receive Communion often, even daily, and in 1910 ruled that children could make their First Communion once they reached the age of reason. He promoted Gregorian chant, reformed the breviary, began the first Code of Canon Law and defended the faith against the errors of his day. He died in August 1914, weeks after the outbreak of the First World War, which he had dreaded. Pius XII canonised him in 1954.",
  prayerTo="Saint Pius, you were a parish priest at heart and wished to bring everyone, even little children, close to Christ in the Eucharist. Pray for children preparing for their First Communion, for catechists and parents, and for me, that I may receive our Lord often and worthily. Amen.",
  penance="Go to Mass and receive Communion today if you can, and pray for children preparing for their First Communion this year.",
  meditation="Holy Communion, he taught, is the shortest and surest way to heaven. How do you prepare to receive it, and how do you give thanks afterwards?",
  query="Pope Pius X portrait painting"),
 "08-23": dict(name="Saint Rose of Lima", dates="1586–1617 · Lima, Peru", match=["rose"],
  history="Isabel Flores de Oliva, called Rose for her beauty as a baby, lived a hidden life of prayer and penance in her family’s garden in Lima, while caring for the sick and poor of the city. A Dominican tertiary, she died at thirty-one and was the first canonised saint of the Americas.",
  life="Rose was born in Lima, then the capital of Spain’s South American empire. Taking Saint Catherine of Siena as her model, she refused offers of marriage and in 1606 became a lay member of the Dominican order. She lived in a tiny hermitage in her parents’ garden and supported her struggling family by fine needlework and by growing flowers to sell. She took into her home sick and destitute people, among them Indians and African slaves, and nursed them herself. Her penances were severe, and her confessors and friends sometimes urged her to moderation. She also endured long years of spiritual darkness. Many in Lima, rich and poor, came to her for prayer and counsel, and when she died in 1617 the whole city mourned. Clement X canonised her in 1671. She is patron of Peru, of Latin America and of the Philippines.",
  prayerTo="Saint Rose, you loved Christ in hiddenness and served Him in the poor of your city. Pray for the peoples of Latin America and for all who are poor and sick. Help me to find God in ordinary work, and to give Him my heart without holding anything back. Amen.",
  penance="Do some quiet, ordinary work today with care, as Rose did her needlework and gardening, and offer what you earn or save to help the sick.",
  meditation="Rose is remembered to have said that apart from the cross there is no other ladder by which we may get to heaven. What cross is in front of you today, and will you climb it or walk around it?",
  query="Saint Rose of Lima painting Murillo"),
 "08-24": dict(name="Saint Bartholomew the Apostle", dates="First century", match=["bartholomew"], efMatch="Bartholomew",
  history="One of the Twelve, generally identified with Nathanael of Cana, whom Philip brought to Jesus. Christ greeted him as an Israelite in whom there is no guile. Tradition holds that he carried the Gospel as far as India and Armenia, and that he was martyred by being flayed alive.",
  life="Bartholomew appears in each list of the apostles beside Philip, and since early times he has been identified with Nathanael in the Gospel of John. When Philip told him they had found the Messiah, Jesus of Nazareth, Nathanael replied, Can anything good come out of Nazareth? Philip simply said, Come and see. Jesus told him He had seen him under the fig tree before Philip called him, and Nathanael answered, Rabbi, you are the Son of God. Later he was among the disciples to whom the risen Christ appeared by the Sea of Tiberias. Eusebius records a tradition that he took the Gospel of Matthew to India. Armenian tradition says he was martyred in Armenia, flayed and beheaded. Artists show him holding a knife, and in Michelangelo’s Last Judgment he holds his own skin. His relics are honoured in Rome on the island in the Tiber.",
  prayerTo="Saint Bartholomew, you came and saw, and confessed Jesus as the Son of God. Pray that I may be without guile, honest with God and with others. When I doubt, bring me back to Christ, and give me courage to bear witness to Him whatever it costs. Amen.",
  penance="Be scrupulously honest today in small things, and ask a friend who is searching to come and see, perhaps to Mass or a talk.",
  meditation="Come and see. Philip did not argue with his friend; he brought him to Jesus. Whom could you invite to come and see?",
  query="Martyrdom of Saint Bartholomew painting Ribera"),
 "08-25": dict(name="Saint Louis", dates="1214–1270 · France", match=["louis"], efMatch="Louis",
  history="Louis IX became King of France at twelve and reigned for over forty years as a just and devout king. He fed the poor at his own table, built the Sainte-Chapelle to house the Crown of Thorns, was loved for his fairness, and twice went on crusade. He died near Tunis in 1270 and was canonised in 1297.",
  life="Louis was crowned in 1226, and his mother, Blanche of Castile, ruled for him until he came of age. She told him she would rather see him dead than guilty of a mortal sin, and he never forgot it. He married Margaret of Provence, and they had eleven children. His friend and biographer Jean de Joinville describes him sitting under an oak at Vincennes to hear the complaints of anyone who came. He reformed the royal courts, forbade judicial duels, founded hospitals including the Quinze-Vingts for the blind, and served the poor with his own hands. In 1239 he acquired the Crown of Thorns and built the Sainte-Chapelle in Paris for it. He led a crusade to Egypt in 1248, was captured and ransomed, and stayed four years in the Holy Land. On a second crusade in 1270 he fell sick at Tunis and died, praying for his people.",
  prayerTo="Saint Louis, king and servant of the poor, you ruled justly and kept your soul free of sin amid wealth and power. Pray for all who govern and judge, for husbands and fathers, and for me, that I may use whatever authority I have for the good of others and the glory of God. Amen.",
  penance="Serve someone poorer than you with your own hands today, perhaps by helping at a soup kitchen or bringing a meal to a neighbour.",
  meditation="Louis wrote to his son that he should have a tender heart for the poor and the suffering. Whose need have you been too busy to notice?",
  query="Saint Louis King of France painting El Greco"),
 "08-27": dict(name="Saint Monica", dates="c. 331–387 · Thagaste and Ostia", match=["monica"],
  history="A Christian woman of North Africa, married to a hot-tempered pagan, whose patience won him to the faith before he died. For years she prayed and wept for her son Augustine, who had abandoned the faith, and followed him to Italy. She saw him baptised in Milan and died soon after at Ostia.",
  life="Almost all we know of Monica comes from her son’s Confessions. She was born at Thagaste in North Africa and married Patricius, a pagan official whose temper and infidelity she bore with patience; he was baptised shortly before his death. Of her children, Augustine caused her the deepest grief, living with a mistress and joining the Manichees. She prayed and wept for him for years, and a bishop she begged for help told her it was not possible that the son of so many tears should be lost. When Augustine left secretly for Rome she followed him there and on to Milan, where she came to love Saint Ambrose. At Easter 387 she saw her son baptised. Waiting to sail home, they stood at a window at Ostia and spoke together of eternal life. Days later she fell ill. She asked only that they remember her at the altar of the Lord, and died aged fifty-six.",
  prayerTo="Saint Monica, patient wife and faithful mother, you never stopped praying for your son. I bring you those I love who have fallen away from the faith. Pray that God will bring them home, and give me your patience, your hope and your perseverance in prayer. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for a family member or friend who has drifted from the faith, and offer a small fast, such as going without a snack or a treat, for them.",
  meditation="It is not possible that the son of so many tears should be lost. For whom are you weeping and praying, and do you still hope?",
  query="Saint Augustine and Saint Monica painting Ary Scheffer"),
 "08-28": dict(name="Saint Augustine of Hippo", dates="354–430 · Thagaste, Milan and Hippo", match=["augustine"], efMatch="Augustine",
  history="A brilliant North African teacher of rhetoric who searched for truth through philosophy and the Manichees, and was converted in Milan in 386 through the prayers of his mother, the preaching of Ambrose and the letters of Saint Paul. As bishop of Hippo he wrote the Confessions and The City of God, and is one of the greatest Doctors of the Church.",
  life="Augustine was born at Thagaste and studied at Carthage, where he took a partner, with whom he had a son, Adeodatus. He joined the Manichees for nine years. Teaching rhetoric at Rome and then Milan, he went to hear Ambrose preach and was slowly won by his reading of the Scriptures. In the summer of 386, in a garden in Milan, he heard a child’s voice chanting, take and read, opened Saint Paul’s letter to the Romans, and his resistance gave way. Ambrose baptised him at Easter 387. Returning to Africa, he lived in community with friends until the people of Hippo pressed him to be ordained priest in 391; he became their bishop a few years later. For over thirty years he preached, judged disputes, cared for the poor and wrote on grace, the Trinity and the Church. He died in 430 as the Vandals besieged Hippo.",
  prayerTo="Saint Augustine, you knew the restless heart and found its rest in God. Pray for all who search for meaning in the wrong places, and for me, that I may not delay any longer in loving God above all things. Help me to seek the truth humbly and to live it with charity. Amen.",
  penance="Read a few pages of the Confessions today, or the passage of Romans 13:11–14 that converted him, and make an honest examination of conscience.",
  meditation="You have made us for yourself, Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you. What are you restless for, and have you brought it to God?",
  query="Saint Augustine in his study painting Botticelli"),
 "08-29": dict(name="The Passion of Saint John the Baptist", dates="First century · Machaerus", match=["beheading", "passion"],
  history="John the Baptist, the forerunner of Christ, told King Herod Antipas that it was not lawful for him to have his brother’s wife. Herod imprisoned him, and at a birthday feast, bound by a rash oath to the daughter of Herodias, had him beheaded. The Church honours his death as a martyrdom for truth and for Christ.",
  life="Saint Mark tells the story in full. Herod Antipas had married Herodias, the wife of his brother, and John rebuked him publicly. Herodias wanted John dead, but Herod feared him, knowing him to be a righteous and holy man, and liked to listen to him even when he was troubled by what he heard. At his birthday banquet her daughter danced for the guests, and Herod swore to give her whatever she asked. Prompted by her mother, she asked for the head of John the Baptist on a dish. Herod was grieved, but would not go back on his oath before his guests. John’s disciples came and laid his body in a tomb. The historian Josephus places his imprisonment in the fortress of Machaerus, east of the Dead Sea. The feast is linked to a church at Sebaste in Samaria, where tradition placed his tomb.",
  prayerTo="Saint John the Baptist, you prepared the way of the Lord and gave your life rather than keep silent about the truth. Pray that I may decrease so that Christ may increase in me. Give me courage to speak the truth with love, even when it is unwelcome, and to keep faith to the end. Amen.",
  penance="Abstain from meat or from a favourite food today in memory of the Forerunner, and keep a promise you have been putting off.",
  meditation="He must increase, but I must decrease. Where is your own pride getting in the way of Christ today?",
  query="Beheading of Saint John the Baptist painting Caravaggio"),
 "08-31": dict(name="Saint Raymond Nonnatus", dates="c. 1204–1240 · Catalonia and North Africa", match=["raymond"], efMatch="Raymond",
  history="A Catalan friar of the Order of Our Lady of Mercy, founded to ransom Christians held captive in North Africa. Tradition holds that he gave himself as a hostage to free others. His name, meaning not born, recalls that he was delivered after his mother died in childbirth, and he is patron of mothers, midwives and the newborn.",
  life="Raymond was born in Catalonia, and tradition says that he was taken alive from his mother’s womb after she had died, hence the name Nonnatus. As a young man he joined the Mercedarians, founded by Saint Peter Nolasco in 1218, whose members vowed to give themselves as hostages, if need be, to free captives. Sent to North Africa with money for ransoms, he is said to have stayed behind as a hostage when the funds ran out, and to have preached to his fellow prisoners. Tradition holds that his captors pierced his lips and padlocked his mouth to stop him preaching. After his release he returned to Spain. The story goes that he was named a cardinal by Gregory IX and set out for Rome, but fell ill and died near Barcelona in 1240. Much of his life is known only through later tradition. His name was entered in the Roman Martyrology in 1657.",
  prayerTo="Saint Raymond, you were given life through your mother’s death and gave your own freedom for captives. Pray for expectant mothers and for all who help bring children into the world, for those held captive, and for me, that I may be willing to give something of myself so that others may be free. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for expectant mothers and for anyone you know who is awaiting a birth, and give to a charity that supports mothers in need or people held captive.",
  meditation="The Mercedarians promised to take the place of captives if there was no other way. Who around you is held captive by debt, addiction or fear, and what could you do for them?",
  query="Saint Raymond Nonnatus painting"),
}
